React Hooks are the morkak Javascript functionS.
Fcebook employees have written these
There are many react hooks but mostly used out of the are : 
useSate(()=>{})
useEffect(()=>{})

import {useState} from "react"

usestate is used to create a state variable
State variable the maintains the state of the component
import { useState } from "react"
const [variablename] = useState([])
variable created using setState cannot be modified like other variables.It is done like:
const [variableName, setVariableName] = useState([]);
setVariableName(filteredList)
Whenever a state variable changes -> React will rerender the component
React is so good at DOM Manipulations
React keep Data layer in sync with UI layer 
so like as just you change restrauntList variable UI will be rerendered to show the updated list on UI 
React uses Reconciliation Algorithm (React Fibre): 
Virtual DOM is not an Actual DOM.It it like an object containing React Elements 
Diff Algorith compares the old virtual DOM and latest virtual DOM 
and then updates the Actual DOM 
In react 16 a new algo to called React Fibre for DOM Update
Virtual DOM is an object representation of DOM
Whenever a state change is there then react compares the virtual DOM and re renders the UI
React is doinf effecient DOM Maniplation because it has virtual DOM hence it is faster
Virtual DOM is the object representation of HTML
//Array destructuring
const ans = useState(resList);
const [res, setRes] = ans 

Monolith Architecture -> Where all service like API, UI, DB etc are there in single project
Microservices Architecture -> Where all services are separate.Like separation of concerns
Single Responsibility Principle is followed in Microservices architecture.
In microservice architecture every service can be written in any language/framework
All the services can be deployed on different domains and ports 
When we call API to show data in our web page there are two approaches : 
1. Page Loads -> api call(some 500 ms) -> and then only page will render 
2. Page loads -> Render(whatever minimum you can) -> make API call -> Re render the page with updated content.
Second approach is better approach as it helps in better UX.
React has one of the best Rendering algos.It re renders very fast

useEffect hook => so Basically once your that particular component will be rendered the the callback function of useEffect will be called.
So if you want anything do be done once render is done, do make useEffect use.

Always use useState hook inside your functional component not outside
Creating state variables at top is a goood practice in functional component
Never use useState variables inside a if-Else, for loop, functions

useEffect(()=>{}, [])
Two argument it takes callback function and a dependency array 

//fetch is functon not given by javascript it is given by JS engine in browsers
const fetchData = async()=>{
    const data = await fetch("url") //fetch returns a promise that's why using async await 
    const json  = await data.json()
    setRestraunt(data.cards[2])
}

useEffect(()=>{fetchData();},[])
Shimmer UI -> fake page
Conditional Rendering
return list.length == 0 ? <Shimmer /> : ()
whenever a value in state variable change react re render that whole component immediately
const [btnName, setBtnName] = useState("Login")
Able to chnage value of this const variable btnName through setBtName because whole component is re rendered and this const variable becomes a new variable hence allowing chnaging the value of constant variable
And uses diff alogo to see change in previous component and then re rendered component
Whenever state variable value chnages -> React triggers the reconciliation process -> Old component and this component compared with diff algo -> and only the part which is different is re rendered quickly
filteredlist and originallist should be two separate local state variables

Dependency Array of use effect: 

-When not passed useEffect(()=>{}) -> useEffect will run on every render of the component
-Empty passed useEffect(()=>{}, []) -> will run on only one very first rendering of component happened 
-varaiblePasses useEffect(()=>{}, [count]).Now here are two cases :
suppose count is a state variable so as soon as count chnages re-render happens useEffect compares the value of count variable before and after re-render and if different useEffect will run
second case is useEffect is a normal variable so if you chnage it's value and somehow you do the re-rendering of your component then as above it will check diff and will run but if you chnage variable value and didn't done re-rendering then no useEffect will run
useEffect watches renders not your variable!!


CORS Policy : corsproxy.io
So different origins means : 
different protocol
different domains
different ports

Now this is a enforcemnet a browser forces and stops browser javascript from reading the response received from an API or call to another origin 
So baiscaly suppose I am loggin on sbi.com. My browser will be having the session cookie of sbi
Now in other tab I opened evil.com
Now evil.com's js will try to make a get request to bank api to fetch my passsowrd and since browser have my cookie it'll automatically attach that to the request
Now Bank API will retrun response to evil.com but Bank API in headers will never say that evil.com is a trusted source to read data from means
So Browser will stop evil.com from reading or accessing this response.

Routing in React
------------------

react-router-dom -> famous librrary
npm -i react-router-dom
For routing we have to create Routing configuration
So go to the file which is parent 
import { createBrowserRouter } from "react-router-dom"
Router Configuartion is a list of path objects 
const appRouter = createBrowserRouter([
    {
        path:"/",
        element: <App />,
        //If React Router gets an error while handling this route, show <Error /> instead.
        errorElement: <Error /> 
    },
    {
        path:"/about",
        element: <About />
    }
])

Now this library has a component routerProvider which provides this routing configuration to our app
import { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from "react-router-dom"

const root = ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById("root"))
root.render(<AppLayout />) now instead of this
root.render(<ReactProvider route={appRouter} />)

📌 rafce and hit enter to have functional component template in vs code 

Fir handling errors, react-router-dom provides hook useRouteError to give more info on errors in routing
import {useRouteError} from "react-router-dom"
const error = useRouteError()
<h1>{error.status}</h1> 

Children Routes
----------------
Basically when we chnage routes we want header and footer to be intact body component should only chnage as per route requested 
import {Outlet} from "react-router-dom"
const App = ()=>{
    return (
        <Header/>
        <Outlet /> //This is a componnet react-router-dom provides for rendering children route components
        <Footer />
    )
}

const appRouter = createBrowserRouter([
    {
        path:"/",
        element: <App />,
        children : [
            {
                path:"/",
                element: <Body />
            },
            {
                path:"/about",
                element: <About />
            },
            {
                path:"/contact",
                element: <Contact />
            },

        ]
        //If React Router gets an error while handling this route, show <Error /> instead.
        errorElement: <Error /> 
    },
   
])

Single Page Application
------------------------
ever use anchor tag for going to a new page while using react as it refreshes the whole page
<li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
For this purpose react-roter-dom provides Link component
import {Link} from "react-router-dom"
<li><Link to="/about">About</Link></li> //It will not reload the page

There are two types of Routing in a web application : 

-Client Side Routing => So here all the components are fetched the page loaded initially and when you naviagte to /about or /contact no network call is made still you see the updated component.
So In Single Page Applications Client side routing is used 
-Server Side Routing => Network callmade and that .html page fetched and refreshed on the screen 

Dynamic Routing 
-----------------

 children : [
            {
                path:"/",
                element: <Body />
            },
            {
                path:"/about",
                element: <About />
            },
            {
                path:"/contact",
                element: <Contact />
            },
            {
                path:"/restraunts/:id", so id will be variable here 
                element: <RestrauntMenu />
            },

        ]
